1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:05,143
In June 2020, a German tech company admitted that one point

2
00:00:05,143 --> 00:00:10,476
nine billion euros on its balance sheet probably did not exist.

3
00:00:11,150 --> 00:00:14,990
That was about a quarter of everything the company said it owned.

4
00:00:15,583 --> 00:00:20,474
Less than two years earlier, Wirecard had been worth more than twenty-four

5
00:00:20,474 --> 00:00:25,629
billion euros, and had taken Commerzbank's place in Germany's top share index.

6
00:00:26,283 --> 00:00:30,095
Its former chief executive, Markus Braun, has now been

7
00:00:30,095 --> 00:00:32,989
on trial in Munich for almost four years.

8
00:00:32,989 --> 00:00:36,943
He denies every charge, and no verdict has been reached.

9
00:00:37,616 --> 00:00:40,505
This is the story told through the paperwork:

10
00:00:40,505 --> 00:00:44,036
the audit, the regulator and the special investigation.

11
00:00:44,036 --> 00:00:47,824
And one document, published two months before the collapse,

12
00:00:47,824 --> 00:00:51,162
already said the key question could not be answered.

13
00:00:51,883 --> 00:00:55,963
Wirecard processed card payments for online shops.

14
00:00:55,963 --> 00:01:01,675
Its share price passed one hundred and ninety euros in September 2018.

15
00:01:02,116 --> 00:01:07,044
At that peak, investors valued it at more than twenty-four billion euros.

16
00:01:07,650 --> 00:01:10,387
Reuters reported that it was worth more than

17
00:01:10,387 --> 00:01:12,814
Deutsche Bank when it joined the index.

18
00:01:13,483 --> 00:01:18,358
To the outside world, it looked like Germany's answer to Silicon Valley.

19
00:01:18,358 --> 00:01:21,066
But a large part of its profit came from

20
00:01:21,066 --> 00:01:24,316
business that other companies did on its behalf.

21
00:01:24,983 --> 00:01:27,751
To understand what went missing, you need

22
00:01:27,751 --> 00:01:31,059
to understand three words: third-party acquiring.

23
00:01:31,583 --> 00:01:35,515
According to KPMG, Wirecard referred customers to three

24
00:01:35,515 --> 00:01:39,447
partner companies, which processed their card payments.

25
00:01:40,116 --> 00:01:43,235
Wirecard booked the fees as its own revenue.

26
00:01:43,235 --> 00:01:47,275
In return, it promised to cover the partners' losses, and

27
00:01:47,275 --> 00:01:51,032
that promise was secured with cash held by a trustee.

28
00:01:51,716 --> 00:01:55,912
KPMG found that the companies running this business produced

29
00:01:55,912 --> 00:01:59,689
the predominant share of the group's operating profit.

30
00:02:00,349 --> 00:02:04,351
So the profit depended on partners outside Wirecard, and

31
00:02:04,351 --> 00:02:08,352
the cash supposedly sat with a trustee outside Wirecard.

32
00:02:08,352 --> 00:02:12,353
Anyone checking had to rely on other people's paperwork.

33
00:02:13,016 --> 00:02:18,271
The first public warnings came in January 2019, when the Financial

34
00:02:18,271 --> 00:02:25,118
Times published allegations of accounting irregularities in Wirecard's Asian business.

35
00:02:25,649 --> 00:02:30,577
Police in Singapore raided Wirecard's offices there in February.

36
00:02:31,183 --> 00:02:36,321
Germany's financial regulator, BaFin, then did something unusual.

37
00:02:36,321 --> 00:02:39,325
On the eighteenth of February 2019, it

38
00:02:39,325 --> 00:02:42,645
banned new bets against Wirecard's shares.

39
00:02:43,316 --> 00:02:46,808
And in April, BaFin filed a criminal complaint

40
00:02:46,808 --> 00:02:51,286
with Munich prosecutors over suspected market manipulation.

41
00:02:51,286 --> 00:02:54,018
AP later reported that the people it

42
00:02:54,018 --> 00:02:57,586
named included two Financial Times journalists.

43
00:02:58,349 --> 00:03:02,322
That investigation was dropped in September 2020.

44
00:03:02,322 --> 00:03:08,077
Prosecutors said the reporting had been, quote, fundamentally accurate.

45
00:03:08,683 --> 00:03:13,099
By then, Wirecard no longer existed as a going concern.

46
00:03:13,683 --> 00:03:19,433
Under pressure, Wirecard hired KPMG for a special investigation.

47
00:03:19,433 --> 00:03:24,285
The report was dated the twenty-seventh of April 2020.

48
00:03:24,749 --> 00:03:29,490
For the years 2016 to 2018, KPMG could neither confirm

49
00:03:29,490 --> 00:03:34,670
nor rule out that the partner business existed as reported.

50
00:03:34,670 --> 00:03:38,533
It used a German word: Untersuchungshemmnis.

51
00:03:38,533 --> 00:03:41,430
An obstacle to the investigation.

52
00:03:42,116 --> 00:03:45,486
The reason, KPMG wrote, was the partners' lack

53
00:03:45,486 --> 00:03:49,296
of willingness to cooperate fully and transparently.

54
00:03:49,983 --> 00:03:53,444
It also reported that about one billion euros of

55
00:03:53,444 --> 00:03:57,121
payments into trust accounts could not be verified.

56
00:03:57,783 --> 00:04:02,542
KPMG was told that the original trustee in Singapore had ended

57
00:04:02,542 --> 00:04:07,070
the relationship at the end of 2019 and stopped responding.

58
00:04:07,070 --> 00:04:12,213
A new trustee took over, with accounts at banks in the Philippines.

59
00:04:12,883 --> 00:04:17,307
Wirecard's own announcement of the report led with a different

60
00:04:17,307 --> 00:04:23,158
line: no incriminating evidence of balance-sheet manipulation had been identified.

61
00:04:23,816 --> 00:04:26,523
Wirecard's auditor was EY.

62
00:04:26,523 --> 00:04:32,563
Its 2019 accounts were due on the eighteenth of June 2020.

63
00:04:33,016 --> 00:04:38,350
That morning, Wirecard announced that EY could not obtain sufficient audit

64
00:04:38,350 --> 00:04:43,035
evidence for one point nine billion euros held in trust accounts.

65
00:04:43,716 --> 00:04:47,287
The company said there were indications that spurious

66
00:04:47,287 --> 00:04:50,790
balance confirmations had been given to the auditor.

67
00:04:50,790 --> 00:04:54,226
If audited accounts were not ready by the next day,

68
00:04:54,226 --> 00:04:58,067
lenders could terminate loans of about two billion euros.

69
00:04:58,816 --> 00:05:01,405
The next day, Markus Braun resigned.

70
00:05:01,405 --> 00:05:06,582
In Manila, two Philippine banks, BPI and BDO, said the documents showing

71
00:05:06,582 --> 00:05:11,544
Wirecard deposits were false, and that Wirecard was not their client.

72
00:05:12,216 --> 00:05:17,410
On the twenty-second of June, Wirecard said there was a, quote, prevailing

73
00:05:17,410 --> 00:05:21,831
likelihood that the one point nine billion euros did not exist.

74
00:05:22,516 --> 00:05:27,465
Braun was arrested that day, and released on five million euros' bail.

75
00:05:28,049 --> 00:05:31,784
On the twenty-fifth, Wirecard filed for insolvency.

76
00:05:31,784 --> 00:05:37,201
Reuters reported it was the first member of the DAX to go out of business.

77
00:05:37,816 --> 00:05:42,269
Fifteen banks had lent it one point seven five billion euros.

78
00:05:42,269 --> 00:05:45,920
Bond investors had lent five hundred million more.

79
00:05:45,920 --> 00:05:49,059
One lender told Reuters: the money is gone.

80
00:05:49,649 --> 00:05:52,448
The shares had lost ninety-eight per cent

81
00:05:52,448 --> 00:05:55,384
since the auditor's refusal a week earlier.

82
00:05:56,049 --> 00:06:00,390
In July 2020, Munich prosecutors had Braun arrested

83
00:06:00,390 --> 00:06:04,306
again, along with two other former executives.

84
00:06:04,849 --> 00:06:09,827
The charges included commercial gang fraud and market manipulation.

85
00:06:09,827 --> 00:06:13,319
Prosecutors alleged that, as early as 2015, the

86
00:06:13,319 --> 00:06:16,737
suspects decided to inflate the balance sheet.

87
00:06:16,737 --> 00:06:21,863
They said a key witness and documents had helped their investigation.

88
00:06:22,549 --> 00:06:26,990
The indictment ran to four hundred and seventy-four pages.

89
00:06:26,990 --> 00:06:32,427
The trial opened on the eighth of December 2022, with three defendants.

90
00:06:33,016 --> 00:06:36,953
Prosecutors put the damage at about three billion euros.

91
00:06:36,953 --> 00:06:38,640
Braun denies everything.

92
00:06:38,640 --> 00:06:42,857
His defence says the business was real, and that others, led

93
00:06:42,857 --> 00:06:47,005
by the former board member Jan Marsalek, stole the profits.

94
00:06:47,683 --> 00:06:50,380
According to the news agency dpa, the

95
00:06:50,380 --> 00:06:54,245
presiding judge has called that argument speculative.

96
00:06:54,916 --> 00:06:58,872
The former Wirecard manager in Dubai, Oliver Bellenhaus,

97
00:06:58,872 --> 00:07:03,110
has confirmed the prosecution's account on essential points.

98
00:07:03,110 --> 00:07:06,430
The former chief accountant denies the charges.

99
00:07:07,116 --> 00:07:10,717
dpa now puts the booked bank balances that cannot

100
00:07:10,717 --> 00:07:13,803
be found at one point seven billion euros.

101
00:07:13,803 --> 00:07:18,212
The insolvency administrator, Michael Jaffé, has still found

102
00:07:18,212 --> 00:07:20,857
no sign that the money ever existed.

103
00:07:21,616 --> 00:07:24,640
Marsalek himself vanished in 2020.

104
00:07:24,640 --> 00:07:30,332
In a London spy trial that ended in 2025, the convicted ring was

105
00:07:30,332 --> 00:07:37,359
described as directed by Marsalek, an alleged Russian agent wanted by Interpol.

106
00:07:37,359 --> 00:07:39,405
He has not faced trial.

107
00:07:40,083 --> 00:07:43,985
dpa reports that the court plans to set a final two-week

108
00:07:43,985 --> 00:07:49,001
deadline for the defence's evidence motions on the eleventh of November.

109
00:07:49,001 --> 00:07:53,112
Dates for closing speeches and a verdict have not been set.

110
00:07:53,783 --> 00:07:56,236
So who has been held to account so far?

111
00:07:56,683 --> 00:08:03,429
In April 2023, Germany's audit watchdog, APAS, found that EY had breached

112
00:08:03,429 --> 00:08:10,174
its professional duties in auditing Wirecard's accounts for 2016 to 2018.

113
00:08:10,174 --> 00:08:13,870
The decisions could still be challenged.

114
00:08:14,549 --> 00:08:18,761
EY was fined five hundred thousand euros, and banned for two

115
00:08:18,761 --> 00:08:23,744
years from taking on new statutory audits of public-interest companies.

116
00:08:23,744 --> 00:08:27,675
Five individual auditors were fined between twenty-three

117
00:08:27,675 --> 00:08:30,622
thousand and three hundred thousand euros.

118
00:08:31,349 --> 00:08:36,970
In September 2024, a Munich civil court ordered Braun and two other former

119
00:08:36,970 --> 00:08:42,666
board members to pay one hundred and forty million euros plus interest, for

120
00:08:42,666 --> 00:08:47,603
an unsecured loan and a bond purchase made without proper checks.

121
00:08:48,349 --> 00:08:51,518
BaFin's president left in early 2021.

122
00:08:51,518 --> 00:08:55,029
The finance ministry said the scandal had

123
00:08:55,029 --> 00:09:00,168
shown that German financial supervision needed reorganising.

124
00:09:00,849 --> 00:09:05,774
And a parliamentary inquiry questioned one hundred and five witnesses.

125
00:09:05,774 --> 00:09:09,081
Its report ran to more than two thousand pages.

126
00:09:09,081 --> 00:09:14,076
Three opposition parties called it a collective failure of supervision.

127
00:09:14,683 --> 00:09:17,527
Here is what we take from the paperwork.

128
00:09:17,527 --> 00:09:19,376
This part is our analysis.

129
00:09:19,816 --> 00:09:20,601
Lesson one.

130
00:09:20,601 --> 00:09:24,952
A confirmation is only as good as the chain that produced it.

131
00:09:24,952 --> 00:09:28,019
EY was given balance confirmations that, by

132
00:09:28,019 --> 00:09:31,372
Wirecard's own account, were probably spurious.

133
00:09:32,049 --> 00:09:32,829
Lesson two.

134
00:09:32,829 --> 00:09:36,161
When most of the profit depends on partners who

135
00:09:36,161 --> 00:09:39,280
won't open their books, that is the finding.

136
00:09:39,280 --> 00:09:43,179
KPMG said so in writing two months before the collapse.

137
00:09:43,849 --> 00:09:44,767
Lesson three.

138
00:09:44,767 --> 00:09:48,082
In our view, a regulator that treats critics as

139
00:09:48,082 --> 00:09:51,045
the problem loses time it cannot get back.

140
00:09:51,045 --> 00:09:56,195
Here, the journalists' reporting was later called fundamentally accurate.

141
00:09:56,849 --> 00:10:01,384
If this sounds familiar, our Patisserie Valerie case file is another

142
00:10:01,384 --> 00:10:05,052
company whose accounts hid what was really in the bank.

143
00:10:05,052 --> 00:10:06,319
It's on screen now.

144
00:10:06,983 --> 00:10:09,812
The Munich verdict could come within months.

145
00:10:09,812 --> 00:10:13,477
Subscribe if you want the follow-up when the court rules.

146
00:10:13,477 --> 00:10:16,049
Our next case file is Greensill Capital.
